## 6.8 Final Conclusion
The age of artificial intelligence has reconfigured economic sovereignty around compute, orchestration, and compliance. For emerging economies, the strategic imperative is not to replicate frontier training ecosystems, but to architect functional sovereignty over inference, agent deployment, human supervision, and cross-border certification. This dissertation establishes the **Sovereign Inference & Orchestration (SIO) framework** as a rigorous, testable, and policy-translatable pathway for compute-efficient AI sovereignty. It formalizes productivity multipliers through human-AI supervision theory, quantifies value capture differentials between domestic inference and foreign API dependency, and demonstrates that regulatory alignment transforms compliance into an export enabler.

Morocco's geographic positioning, renewable energy expansion, multilingual talent base, and competitive operational costs provide a structurally advantageous foundation for agent economy development. The phased roadmap (2026–2040), institutional architecture, and conditional validation pipeline offer a replicable model for middle-income economies navigating AI infrastructure dependency. 

The transition from theoretical framework to empirical implementation requires coordinated public-private-academic collaboration, transparent validation protocols, and adaptive governance. If executed with academic rigor and policy discipline, this pathway will enable Morocco to evolve from a consumer of foreign intelligence into a producer and exporter of scalable digital cognitive labor.

The goal is not technological maximalism. It is **strategic sufficiency**: sovereignty defined not by scale, but by control, compliance, and value capture. This dissertation provides the theoretical architecture, methodological blueprint, and policy translation mechanisms to make that objective empirically achievable.

